---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro'
import { reportStats } from '../../utils/reports'

const s = reportStats()
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is design drift?',
    a: 'Design drift is the gap that grows between a design system and the code that ships: hardcoded colors and spacing instead of tokens, near-duplicate components, and tokens replaced by their raw values. Each change looks harmless; together they make the product inconsistent and expensive to rebrand.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What causes design drift?',
    a: 'Deadlines, copy-pasted snippets, missing tokens for values teams need, and increasingly AI coding assistants, which write literal values unless they are given the design system as context.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do you detect design drift?',
    a: 'Scan the code for values and components that bypass the design system, ignoring literals that are correct (icons, email templates, tests, layout math). Buoy does this from a GitHub URL at buoy.design/scan or with `npx @buoy-design/cli drift check`.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do you prevent design drift?',
    a: 'Check new code where it arrives: pull request reviews that flag only added lines and suggest the existing token, a pre-commit check on staged lines, and giving AI coding assistants the tokens through an MCP server.',
  },
]
---

<GuideLayout
  title="What is design drift?"
  seoTitle="What Is Design Drift? Causes, Examples and How to Prevent It | Buoy"
  description="Design drift is the gap between a design system and the code that ships. What causes it, what it looks like in real codebases, and how to detect and prevent it."
  keywords="design drift, what is design drift, design system drift, UI inconsistency, design debt, design system governance"
  slug="what-is-design-drift"
  updated={s.updated}
  answer="Design drift is the growing gap between a design system and the code that ships: literal colors and spacing where tokens belong, near-duplicate components, and tokens swapped for their raw values. It is detected by scanning code for values that bypass the system, and prevented by checking new code in pull requests."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <h2>What it looks like</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hardcoded values.</strong> <code>color: #2563eb</code> where <code>var(--color-primary)</code> exists, or <code>p-[13px]</code> between <code>p-3</code> and <code>p-4</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Token regressions.</strong> A pull request replaces <code>var(--space-4)</code> with <code>16px</code>. It renders identically today and stops following the scale tomorrow.</li>
    <li><strong>Near-duplicate components.</strong> <code>PrimaryButton</code>, <code>ButtonPrimary</code> and <code>CTAButton</code>, each edited separately.</li>
    <li><strong>Repeated one-offs.</strong> The same gray in forty files, which is a missing token rather than forty mistakes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It is common in good codebases. Across {s.scoredCount} well-known open-source projects, Buoy found
    {s.findings.toLocaleString()} hardcoded-value findings in {s.components.toLocaleString()} components
    (<a href="/reports">reports</a>).
  </p>

  <h2>Why it happens</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Deadlines.</strong> A literal is faster than finding the token.</li>
    <li><strong>Missing tokens.</strong> If the scale has no value between 12px and 16px, someone writes 14px.</li>
    <li><strong>Copy-paste</strong> from other files, Stack Overflow and design tool exports.</li>
    <li><strong>AI coding assistants.</strong> They write working code with literal values unless they are given the tokens.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>
    Each instance is small. The cost appears at the next rebrand, dark mode or accessibility fix, when a change that
    should touch one token touches hundreds of files, and some are missed.
  </p>

  <h2>What is not drift</h2>
  <p>
    Not every literal is a problem. SVG icons, email templates, tests, layout math such as <code>calc()</code>,
    and deliberate choices a comment explains are correct as they are. Flagging them is how drift tools lose the
    team's trust.
  </p>

  <h2>Detect and prevent it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Measure it: <a href="/scan">scan a repository</a> or run <code>npx @buoy-design/cli show health</code>.</li>
    <li>Fix the cheap wins: literals that exactly match an existing token.</li>
    <li>Stop new drift at the pull request, in a pre-commit hook, and in AI assistants via <code>npx @buoy-design/cli mcp install</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>More: <a href="/guides/how-to-audit-a-design-system">how to audit a design system</a> and <a href="/guides/design-system-health-score">how design system health is measured</a>.</p>
</GuideLayout>
